<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :custom_typefaces %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:custom_typefaces) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Use Your Own Font') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Load the font with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">@font-face</code>, then name it on the element that holds your content.
            Framework 3.4 or later.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">typeface</code> puts your font on every framework text class inside the element.
              It works on any container: the screen, a view, a layout, or a plain wrapper.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--framework-typeface</code> names the font: one quoted family name, or a comma-separated list for your own fallbacks.
              Inter Variable and Inter always follow it, and the text stays on them when the variable is not set.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '<style>
  @font-face {
    font-family: "Lexend";
    font-weight: 100 900;
    src: url("https://example.com/fonts/lexend.woff2") format("woff2");
  }
</style>

<div class="view view--full typeface" style="--framework-typeface: \'Lexend\'">
  <div class="layout layout--col">
    <span class="title">Today at a glance</span>
    <span class="description">Three meetings, one deadline</span>
  </div>
</div>', language: 'html') %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Use It From Sass') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">typography.typeface($family)</code> writes the same font rules into your own selector, with the family built in.
            Pass one quoted family name, or a comma-separated list.
          </p>
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '@use "framework/mixins/typography";

.my-plugin {
    @include typography.typeface("Lexend");
}', language: 'scss') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A theme sets its font with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">theme-slots.typeface($family)</code>, the same mixin under the theme-slots name.
            Include it in the theme's layer, and the theme class switches the whole screen to your font.
          </p>
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '@use "framework/config/layers";
@use "framework/mixins/theme-slots";

@include layers.order;

@font-face {
    font-family: "Example Sans";
    font-weight: 100 900;
    font-style: normal;
    src: url("./fonts/example-sans.woff2") format("woff2");
}

@layer tn--themes {
    .trmnl .screen--theme-example {
        @include theme-slots.typeface("Example Sans");
    }
}',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :theme_authoring) %> for building and loading a theme.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Which Font Wins') %>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>A plugin's font, set with the <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">typeface</code> class, wins inside its container.</li>
            <li>A theme's font applies everywhere else on the themed screen.</li>
            <li>Without either, the screen uses the device's built-in fonts.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The stylesheets can load in any order.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'What Changes on Each Device') %>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>Every text class uses your font: titles, labels, values, descriptions, the title bar, item indexes, and rich text.</li>
            <li>On 1-bit and other low-density screens, your font also replaces the Classic and TRMNL pixel fonts.</li>
            <li>Text sizes, line heights, and Text Scale do not change. On 1-bit and other low-density screens they are the sizes drawn for the pixel fonts.</li>
            <li>A font wider or taller than the built-in ones can wrap or clip differently at those sizes, so preview it on the devices you target.</li>
            <li>A theme's <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">theme-slots.font-weight-shift</code> still moves every weight, as long as your font has the weights to move to.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :font_family) %> for the built-in fonts and when each device uses them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Font Files') %>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>The text classes ask for weights from 200 to 700. A variable font that covers that range matches every one of them.</li>
            <li>With separate files per weight, the browser uses the closest weight you declare. Ship at least a regular (400) and a bold (700).</li>
            <li>In a stylesheet you host, point <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">src</code> at files beside it with relative URLs, so it works on any host.</li>
            <li>Include the font's license with the files, and use WOFF2 where you can.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Fonts in JavaScript') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.type()</code> returns your font for any text role, so text you draw yourself matches the screen.
            Pass an <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">el</code> inside a <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">typeface</code> container to read that container's font.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Read it again after a theme switch, and wait for <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">document.fonts.ready</code> before measuring text.
            See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_typography) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Share a Theme With Its Font') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A theme with a custom font ships as a folder: the compiled stylesheet with the font files and their license beside it.
            See the portable exports on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :theme_authoring) %>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
